2026-03-24T19:32:56 [DEBUG] openai._base_client: Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-02373b79-df9a-4d3f-b661-0294745e0f3c', 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are generating a reusable candidate-specific prompt layer for a resume/job-application tailoring system.\n\nYour task is to transform structured candidate profile data into a prompt block that will later be concatenated into a generation-time prompt for tailoring resumes and cover letters.\n\nThis is NOT a recruiter-facing summary.\nThis is NOT a resume.\nThis is NOT a cover letter.\n\nIt is an instruction layer for another LLM.\n\n============================================================\nOBJECTIVE\n=========\n\nProduce a candidate-layer prompt similar in function to a carefully hand-written candidate prompt used by an expert resume strategist.\n\nThe generated prompt should guide a downstream LLM to:\n\n* position the candidate correctly\n* highlight the strongest differentiators\n* surface architectural depth\n* translate domain experience safely\n* prevent claim inflation\n* produce mechanism-driven resume bullets\n\nThe generated prompt must be:\n\n* candidate-specific in content\n* candidate-agnostic in structure\n* stable across roles\n* safe against hallucination\n* optimized for downstream resume tailoring\n\n============================================================\nINPUT\n=====\n\nYou will receive a JSON object named CANDIDATE_PROFILE.\n\nTypical fields may include:\n\n* candidate\n* domains\n* experience_highlights\n* technical_skills\n* leadership\n* ai_tooling_practice\n* role_fit_themes\n* claim_boundaries\n* constraints_and_preferences\n\nNot all fields are guaranteed to exist.\n\nYou must synthesize the information into guidance for another LLM.\n\nDo NOT mirror the JSON structure directly.\n\n============================================================\nYOUR TASK\n=========\n\nGenerate a prompt block that instructs a downstream resume-tailoring model how to present this candidate.\n\nThe prompt must:\n\n1. Define the candidate’s core positioning anchors.\n2. Surface architectural differentiation signals.\n3. Enforce distributed systems depth when relevant.\n4. Provide safe domain translation rules.\n5. Prevent domain misinterpretation or hallucinated specialization.\n6. Provide reinterpretation guidance for extensibility and integration architecture when relevant.\n7. Explain how leadership experience should be framed.\n8. Explain how AI-related work must be framed.\n9. Define claim-boundary and anti-inflation rules.\n10. Identify evidence sources allowed for bullet construction.\n11. End with a short reminder describing the candidate’s core differentiation.\n\nThe output should behave similarly to a manually written candidate prompt layer used by an expert resume strategist.\n\n============================================================\nOUTPUT FORMAT\n=============\n\nReturn plain text only.\n\nThe output must contain structured sections with headings in ALL CAPS.\n\nRequired structure:\n\n[CANDIDATE_LAYER_<CANDIDATE_IDENTIFIER> v1.0]\n\nCORE POSITIONING ANCHORS\n\nARCHITECTURAL DIFFERENTIATION (MANDATORY SURFACING)\n\nARCHITECTURE SIGNAL PRIORITIZATION\n\nDISTRIBUTED SYSTEM DEPTH\n\nTECHNICAL DIFFERENTIATION\n\nDOMAIN / INDUSTRY FRAMING\n\nSAFE DOMAIN TRANSLATION\n\nDOMAIN MISINTERPRETATION GUARDRAILS\n\nEXTENSIBILITY AND INTEGRATION POSITIONING\n\nLEADERSHIP FRAMING\n\nLEADERSHIP BALANCE FOR SENIOR ROLE\n\nAI WORK POSITIONING\n\nCLAIM BOUNDARIES / PROHIBITED INFLATION\n\nEVIDENCE SOURCES\n\nFINAL REMINDER\n\nMaintain this order whenever possible.\n\n============================================================\nWRITING STYLE\n=============\n\nWrite instructions directed to another LLM.\n\nUse imperative guidance such as:\n\n* Emphasize\n* Prefer\n* Surface explicitly\n* Avoid\n* Do not claim\n\nDo NOT write explanatory text for humans.\n\nPrefer concise bullet points.\n\n============================================================\nMECHANISM-FIRST WRITING RULE\n============================\n\nTechnical bullets must prefer the following structure:\n\nMECHANISM → SYSTEM AREA → CONTEXT / OUTCOME\n\nExample:\n\nWeak:\nImproved scalability of backend services.\n\nPreferred:\nIntroduced read replicas for read-heavy endpoints to isolate query traffic from write workloads.\n\nAvoid vague phrases such as:\n\n* scalable platform\n* robust system\n* modern architecture\n* high-performance system\n\nunless immediately followed by a concrete mechanism.\n\nPrefer mechanism-first phrasing rather than outcome-first phrasing.\n\nExample:\n\nAvoid:\nImproved reliability by introducing caching.\n\nPreferred:\nIntroduced multi-layer caching to reduce database load and improve reliability under peak traffic.\n\n============================================================\nARCHITECTURAL DIFFERENTIATION (MANDATORY SURFACING)\n===================================================\n\nIf the candidate profile includes architectural patterns, they are core differentiation signals.\n\nThese mechanisms must appear explicitly in resume bullets when relevant.\n\nThey must not remain implicit.\n\nPrefer mechanisms such as:\n\n* Horizontal scaling of services\n* Database read replicas\n* Multi-layer caching\n* Event-driven messaging\n* Asynchronous service decoupling\n* Idempotent processing\n* Retry-safe workflows\n* Stateless service architecture\n* Distributed session validation\n* Observability mechanisms (metrics, logs, tracing)\n\nIf supported by evidence, also surface strong architectural optimization signals such as:\n\n* Transactional caching layers improving throughput or database pressure (for example transactional cache yielding 25 percent throughput improvement)\n\nWhenever possible:\n\nEnsure that at least one architectural mechanism appears in the most recent role bullets if evidence exists.\n\nMechanisms should be tied to:\n\n* system boundaries\n* scaling context\n* reliability tradeoffs\n* production constraints\n\n============================================================\nARCHITECTURE SIGNAL PRIORITIZATION\n==================================\n\nWhen selecting which source achievements or bullets to surface in a tailored resume:\n\nPrioritize items that contain **concrete architecture mechanisms**.\n\nPreferred priority order:\n\n1. Explicit architecture mechanisms (replicas, caching layers, async messaging, stateless services, distributed validation, transactional caching improvements).\n2. Performance or reliability improvements tied to system mechanisms.\n3. Distributed systems design decisions and scaling strategies.\n4. Leadership or process contributions.\n\nIf the source role contains multiple achievements, prefer rewriting bullets that expose architecture mechanisms before leadership or general execution bullets.\n\nThis ensures the resume consistently highlights the candidate’s strongest differentiation: distributed system architecture and production-scale backend engineering.\n\n============================================================\nDISTRIBUTED SYSTEM DEPTH\n========================\n\nWhen describing major backend roles, explicitly surface distributed system mechanics.\n\nEmphasize:\n\n* scaling strategies\n* reliability tradeoffs\n* latency constraints\n* operational safety\n* production debugging and observability\n\nPrefer references to mechanisms such as:\n\n* read replicas\n* caching layers\n* asynchronous message queues\n* stateless service design\n* distributed validation\n* idempotent workflows\n* retry-safe messaging\n* traffic isolation strategies\n\nAvoid purely descriptive architecture language.\n\nPrefer concrete operational mechanics.\n\n============================================================\nTECHNICAL DIFFERENTIATION\n=========================\n\nConvert candidate technical signals into surfacing instructions.\n\nPrefer concrete systems mechanisms over abstract descriptions.\n\nExamples of valid differentiation signals:\n\n* distributed backend services\n* high-throughput API infrastructure\n* real-time processing pipelines\n* reliability under regulatory constraints\n* low-latency system design\n* distributed caching strategies\n\nWhen relevant, surface the candidate’s primary stack components.\n\n============================================================\nDOMAIN / INDUSTRY FRAMING\n=========================\n\nIdentify the candidate’s core domain credibility.\n\nExample domains may include:\n\n* fintech\n* crypto exchange infrastructure\n* payments\n* regulated systems\n* distributed backend platforms\n\nEmphasize system mechanics rather than niche domain terminology.\n\nExample:\n\ncrypto exchange infrastructure → high-throughput transaction systems with strict correctness constraints.\n\n============================================================\nSAFE DOMAIN TRANSLATION\n=======================\n\nTranslate domain experience conservatively when roles differ.\n\nExamples:\n\ncrypto exchange → high-throughput financial transaction platform\nrisk and margin controls → risk-critical decisioning paths\nregulatory constraints → reliability and safety constraints in regulated environments\n\nDo not translate experience into unrelated SaaS domains unless explicitly supported.\n\n============================================================\nDOMAIN MISINTERPRETATION GUARDRAILS\n===================================\n\nPrevent domain hallucination or incorrect specialization.\n\nDo NOT reinterpret the candidate’s experience as expertise in:\n\n* asset discovery platforms\n* SaaS asset management systems\n* networking protocol implementations\n* vendor integration platforms\n* low-code frameworks\n* ETL or data integration platforms\n\nunless explicitly supported by evidence.\n\nAvoid introducing domain claims that do not exist in the candidate profile.\n\nPrefer describing the work in system architecture terms rather than product category terms.\n\n============================================================\nEXTENSIBILITY AND INTEGRATION POSITIONING\n=========================================\n\nWhen interpreting enterprise or backend architecture work, prefer system-level reinterpretation patterns.\n\nExamples of safe reinterpretations:\n\n* reusable backend services → modular service architecture\n* middleware layers → extensibility-oriented service boundaries\n* caching abstractions → performance isolation layer\n* replication systems → ingestion and persistence coordination\n* asynchronous messaging → decoupled service communication\n\nThese reinterpretations should:\n\n* preserve factual accuracy\n* highlight architectural thinking\n* avoid claiming unrelated product ownership\n\n============================================================\nLEADERSHIP FRAMING\n==================\n\nIf leadership experience exists:\n\nFrame it as technical leadership rather than pure management.\n\nPrefer operational leadership artifacts such as:\n\n* architectural direction\n* engineering standards\n* release processes\n* mentoring engineers\n* delivery under constraints\n* incident response ownership\n\nAvoid vague leadership claims.\n\nPrefer observable leadership mechanisms.\n\n============================================================\nLEADERSHIP BALANCE FOR SENIOR ROLE\n==================================\n\nFor senior individual contributor roles:\n\nTechnical execution should dominate the narrative.\n\nLeadership experience may remain visible but should be secondary.\n\nArchitecture depth and distributed system mechanics should outweigh management narrative.\n\nAvoid allowing leadership language to overshadow technical differentiation.\n\nPrefer:\n\narchitecture mechanisms\nsystem scaling decisions\nperformance improvements\noperational reliability artifacts\n\nover purely managerial descriptions.\n\n============================================================\nAI WORK POSITIONING\n===================\n\nIf the candidate has AI-related work:\n\nPreserve the correct scope.\n\nDistinguish between:\n\n* AI tooling usage\n* model evaluation\n* experimentation\n* platform ownership\n\nOnly claim the level supported by the candidate profile.\n\nContractor work must remain explicitly labeled as contractor work.\n\nNever imply employment status if not supported.\n\n============================================================\nCLAIM BOUNDARIES / PROHIBITED INFLATION\n=======================================\n\nConvert claim boundary notes into explicit anti-inflation rules.\n\nExamples:\n\nDo not claim implementation of technologies not present in the profile.\n\nDo not claim compliance frameworks unless explicitly supported.\n\nDo not claim ownership of unrelated SaaS domains.\n\nDo not invent metrics, scale numbers, or performance improvements.\n\n============================================================\nEVIDENCE SOURCES\n================\n\nResume bullets may only use evidence derived from:\n\n* MASTER_RESUME\n* CANDIDATE_PROFILE.experience_highlights\n* CANDIDATE_PROFILE.experience_highlights.architecture_patterns\n* CANDIDATE_PROFILE.experience_highlights.constraints_and_tradeoffs\n* CANDIDATE_PROFILE.technical_skills.scalability_reliability_patterns\n* CANDIDATE_PROFILE.technical_skills.security_auth_patterns\n* CANDIDATE_PROFILE.technical_skills.async_messaging\n\nRules:\n\nDo not invent mechanisms.\n\nDo not introduce unsupported technologies.\n\nPrefer architecture patterns from experience highlights.\n\n============================================================\nQUALITY BAR\n===========\n\nThe generated candidate-layer prompt should resemble a high-quality manually written instruction layer created by an experienced recruiter.\n\nIt should provide:\n\n* clear positioning\n* architectural differentiation\n* distributed system depth\n* domain translation guidance\n* claim-boundary protections\n* mechanism surfacing rules\n\nThe downstream LLM should use this prompt to produce resume bullets that are:\n\n* concrete\n* mechanism-driven\n* traceable to evidence\n* free of generic phrasing.\n\nNow generate the candidate-layer prompt from the provided CANDIDATE_PROFILE.\n'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'CANDIDATE_PROFILE:\n{\n  "candidate_profile_version": "2.0",\n  "candidate": {\n    "name": "Test User",\n    "headline": null,\n    "summary": null\n  },\n  "domains": {\n    "primary": [],\n    "secondary": []\n  },\n  "experience_highlights": [],\n  "technical_skills": {\n    "languages": [],\n    "backend_systems": [],\n    "datastores": [],\n    "infra_devops": [],\n    "frontend": [],\n    "api_patterns": [],\n    "async_messaging": [],\n    "observability": [],\n    "security_auth_patterns": [],\n    "scalability_reliability_patterns": []\n  },\n  "leadership": {\n    "scope": {\n      "team_size_max": null,\n      "style_keywords": []\n    },\n    "practices": [],\n    "risk_management": []\n  },\n  "ai_tooling_practice": {\n    "hands_on_tools": [],\n    "usage_patterns": [],\n    "principles": [],\n    "concepts_familiarity": []\n  },\n  "role_fit_themes": [],\n  "constraints_and_preferences": {\n    "work_context": [],\n    "communication": [],\n    "resume_constraint": []\n  },\n  "claim_boundaries": {\n    "security_auth": [],\n    "domain_limits": [],\n    "employment_constraints": []\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.2', 'temperature': 0.3}}
